00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:40.469
In our episode last week, we introduced you
to a particular type of relationship between

00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:45.595
two creatures called epibiosis, which is when
one organism takes up residence

00:00:45.595 --> 00:00:47.829
on the surface of another.

00:00:47.829 --> 00:00:54.389
And we asked which one you would prefer to be,
the one living on, or the one being lived upon.

00:00:54.389 --> 00:00:58.792
And there are plenty of pros and cons to either
lifestyle, but these interactions can vary

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:03.739
a lot based on the species involved. So we
promised to dive a little deeper into one

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:08.810
specific example of epibiosis where it might
be a little bit easier to decide because choosing

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.783
wrong means death.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:21.030
This is kentrophoros, a ciliate that James—our
master of microscopes—had been searching

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:27.399
for, receiving samples from all over the world
in the hopes of finding it gliding around.

00:01:27.399 --> 00:01:34.577
And finally, it turned up in some sand samples
he received from the Adriatic coast of Italy.

00:01:34.577 --> 00:01:38.890
When you first look at it, this kentrophoros
probably doesn’t seem particularly special.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:44.079
It’s just slowly gliding around the slide,
looking a bit more like a worm than a fascinating

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:46.265
case study in epibiosis.

00:01:46.265 --> 00:01:49.810
But there are two things that the kentrophoros
is famous for.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.990
The first is its lack of a mouth.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:56.609
And the second is its coat of bacteria.

00:01:56.609 --> 00:02:03.142
As we zoom in, you can see those bacteria
poking out on one side of the ciliate’s body.

00:02:03.142 --> 00:02:08.520
Scientists have actually estimated that one
Kentrophoros can have around 4,500 individual

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.600
bacteria living on it.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:15.920
At least 15 kentrophoros species have been
described, but they can look very different

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.112
from one another.

00:02:17.112 --> 00:02:20.211
Some are like ours—flat and ribbon-like.

00:02:20.211 --> 00:02:22.684
Others are built more like a tube.

00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:26.720
Some have their bacterial coat laid out on
one side of their body, while others tuck

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.780
the bacteria into pockets made out of their
own self .

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.391
The fact that these different species look
so different from one another—and that the

00:02:34.391 --> 00:02:39.730
only things really tying them together were
mouthlessness and bacterial companions—meant

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:45.795
that scientists had to ask the question: are
these different species actually related?

00:02:45.795 --> 00:02:49.129
It wouldn’t be the first, or last, time
that we’d been led astray

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:51.480
by a few coincidental similarities.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.879
But when they studied the Kentrophoros’
DNA, they found that the species are in

00:02:55.879 --> 00:03:00.900
fact related to each other, suggesting that
they may have evolved this relationship with

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:06.769
their bacterial friends long ago and spread
the partnership all over the world.

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:13.060
When you look at the bacteria on their own,
you can see their rod shape more clearly.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:17.510
These bacteria are primarily thiotrophs, meaning
that they live on sulfur.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:21.050
When researchers dug into the genetics of
these bacteria, they found that they were

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:27.670
all members of the same clade, Gammaproteobacteria—a
type of bacteria that includes more famous

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:29.560
sulfur bacteria like Beggiatoa.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:34.606
But additional work found evidence of another
type of bacteria in that coat as well called

00:03:34.606 --> 00:03:41.196
Muribaculaceae—a branch of bacteria that
includes residents of our own guts.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.370
Now we mentioned earlier that James found these
Kentrophoros in samples that came from the

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.264
Adriatic Coast, and that’s no accident.

00:03:49.264 --> 00:03:55.170
Kentrophoros are usually found in marine sediments,
and that is thanks in part to the thiotrophic

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.920
bacteria that live on their surface.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:03.920
Their relationship reminds us a bit of Paramecium
bursaria and the green algae Chlorella that

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:09.579
live as endosymbionts inside of vacuoles specially
designed to hold them.

00:04:09.579 --> 00:04:15.593
Chlorella love sun, using it to drive their
photosynthesis so they can make their own food.

00:04:15.593 --> 00:04:20.910
And so, to suit the needs of their internal
residents, Paramecium bursaria are also drawn

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.166
to well-lit areas.

00:04:23.166 --> 00:04:28.000
It is an exchange that works very well for 
both the algae and the paramecium.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.030
The algae gets the safety of the paramecium’s
body, and the paramecium gets some of the

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:36.800
nutritional benefit of whatever the algae
has made.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.740
The Kentrophoros’ relationship with their
thiotrophic bacteria is similar, except that

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:46.960
the bacteria live on the Kentrophoros instead
of inside them.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:52.710
And instead of being drawn to light, the Kentrophoros
are drawn instead to that band of water that

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:58.370
hovers above the sediment where oxygen is
low and there is just enough hydrogen sulfide

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.610
for their bacteria to convert into food.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:06.810
By letting themself be drawn to these areas,
the Kentrophoros benefits as well, taking

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:10.463
in some of the metabolites made by the bacteria.

00:05:10.463 --> 00:05:16.770
And it seems that this exchange has paid off
enough for both the bacteria and the Kentrophoros.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:21.419
The kentrophoros’ cilia is distributed in
a way to make sure that there are specific

00:05:21.419 --> 00:05:25.060
places kept free and clear for their surface
tenants.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:30.259
And the pockets we described some kentrophoros
species as having maximize the amount of space

00:05:30.259 --> 00:05:33.729
available for the bacteria to attach themselves
too.

00:05:33.729 --> 00:05:40.020
Meanwhile, the kentrophoros’ bacteria divide
in a way that is not typically seen, but

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.460
that makes sense when you remember how it
lives.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:48.085
Typically, a rod-shaped bacteria would divide
down the middle, along its shorter axis.

00:05:48.085 --> 00:05:53.509
But these bacteria tear apart down the longer
axis, breaking apart into a sort of “V”

00:05:53.509 --> 00:05:55.830
shape that then splits into two.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:57.740
And why would it do that?

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:02.380
Because it lets them stay attached to their
host as they divide, hooked on through the

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:07.454
root of that “V” until it’s ready to
completely separate into two.

00:06:07.454 --> 00:06:13.520
And this all seems to be fairly balanced, 
well-managed relationship of the microcosmos.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:18.849
The bacteria gets access to sulfur, the Kentrophoros
gets access to nutrients.

00:06:18.849 --> 00:06:21.423
What could possibly go wrong?

00:06:22.175 --> 00:06:25.278
Well, a lot, if you are the bacteria.

00:06:25.278 --> 00:06:30.790
Because sometimes, the Kentrophoros gets hungry…
too hungry.

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:34.860
A few metabolites from their bacterial buddies
just aren’t enough anymore.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:36.906
But the bacteria themselves?

00:06:36.906 --> 00:06:40.449
Well, that is a handy snack to have on hand.

00:06:40.449 --> 00:06:44.758
The kentrophoros may be mouthless, but it
is not harmless.

00:06:44.758 --> 00:06:48.310
It has a trick up its sleeve: phagocytosis.

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:52.830
It simply absorbs the bacteria into itself
and begins to eat.

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:57.770
In some descriptions of the relationship between
Kentrophoros and bacteria, the scientists

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:03.490
Bland Finlay and Tom Fenchel have used the
phrase “kitchen garden” to describe them,

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:06.800
which is a quaint image from the Kentrophoros’
point of view.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:12.629
It's kinda sweet, after all, to imagine the ciliate
raising a field of bacteria, fertilizing it

00:07:12.629 --> 00:07:17.216
with trips through anoxic waters and tending
to its needs.

00:07:17.216 --> 00:07:22.720
But from the bacteria’s point of view, it
must feel like a bit of a betrayal.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:28.259
Like the whole ground beneath their feet has
just opened up and swallowed them whole.

00:07:28.259 --> 00:07:33.770
And in our imagination, that seems like the
far worse place to exist.

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:37.449
If you were to ask the simple question of
“who would you rather be,” the answer

00:07:37.449 --> 00:07:39.060
feels to us like an easy one.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:43.410
It seems much more appealing to be the creature
who chooses to consume the other, not the

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:48.710
one who goes from friend to phagocytosed without
warning.

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:51.949
But maybe we are being
too human about this.

00:07:51.949 --> 00:07:57.949
The relationship between these bacteria and
the ciliate has gone on for a long time, covering

00:07:57.949 --> 00:08:00.720
oceans on opposite sides of the planet.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.550
There is something about this that must work
for the bacteria, even if we don’t have

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:09.000
the capacity to imagine it working for us.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:13.919
Perhaps there is something to this life—attached
to the side of another creature’s body,

00:08:13.919 --> 00:08:19.509
surviving as countless copies of yourself
endure—perhaps there’s something to all

00:08:19.509 --> 00:08:22.449
that makes sense when it’s all you’ve
known.

00:08:22.449 --> 00:08:29.060
And perhaps it is our life…our way of imagining
ourselves as whole, complete individuals rather

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:34.996
than as a single note in the broader symphony
of our species that is truly bizarre.

00:08:38.308 --> 00:08:43.050
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

